<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Asano clan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Asano clan was a Japanese warrior family that rose through its ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a>, whose wife Nene had been adopted into the family. From 1619 its main line ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiroshima">Hiroshima</a> domain until 1871, while a branch held Akō, whose lord's forced suicide in 1701 led to the revenge of the forty-seven <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ronin">ronin</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/asano-clan.jpg" alt="Asano clan" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alex Tora · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alex_K_Hiroshima_Asano_(color).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Asano were a branch of the Toki clan, which descended from Minamoto no Yorimitsu of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seiwa-genji">Seiwa Genji</a> and took its name from the Toki District of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mino-province">Mino Province</a>. Minamoto no Mitsuhira, adopted by his uncle Minamoto no Mitsumoto, founded the Toki clan as Toki Mitsuhira; his first son Toki Mitsuyuki continued it, while his second son, Toki Mitsutoki, settled at Asano in the Toki District and took its name, becoming the founder of the Asano clan. The family's inherited character was 長.</p><p>Asano Nagamasa was born a son of Yasui Shigetsugu, lord of Miyago castle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owari-province">Owari Province</a> and a descendant of the Hatakeyama and Ashikaga lines. He was adopted by his maternal uncle Asano Nagakatsu, lord of Asano castle, and succeeded him as the fourteenth head of the clan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The family rose when Nagamasa's sister married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a>, and Nagamasa fought for his brother-in-law in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, as did his eldest son Asano Yoshinaga. Yoshinaga held the Kishū Domain in Kii, rated at 376,000 koku. His brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asano-nagaakira">Asano Nagaakira</a> succeeded him there and was moved to the Hiroshima Domain in Aki, rated at 426,500 koku, becoming the first Asano lord of Hiroshima. The third son, Asano Nagashige, held in turn Makabe, Mooka and Kasama.</p><p>Nagashige's eldest son Asano Naganao was transferred from Kasama to the Akō Domain in Harima, rated at 53,000 koku, in 1645, and his grandson Asano Naganori was the lord of the Forty-seven rōnin. Other branches held the domains of Hiroshima-Shinden, Miyoshi and Tōjō, and a line descended from Nagamasa's cousin Asano Tadayoshi held Mihara in Bingo. The last lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiroshima">Hiroshima</a> was Asano Nagakoto, who had first led the Hiroshima-Shinden branch as Asano Nagaoki. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a> and the abolition of the han system the Asano clan became part of the new nobility.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Asano clan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
